  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 319 AM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nA Georgia Scorcher, for sure! Conditions in the long term, beginning \nSunday night and continuing through the week will be hot. Models \nshow high agreement in upper level ridging peaking on Monday \nbefore northern shortwaves help to suppress the ridge. What this \nmeans for the area is a suppression of storm coverage on Monday \nand a slight uptrend in PoPs each afternoon from then on. \nTemperatures will follow with the highest temps on Monday. \nDepending on exact cloud cover, which will likely be low given \nsuppressed convection, max temps could reach 100 across much of \nthe area. This would include the Atlanta Metro, and would be the \nfirst time ATL has hit 100 since the end of last July (only the \n3rd time this decade). Dewpoints will only remain slightly \nelevated, part of the reason temps are able to get as high as they\nare, sitting around 70F. The resultant heat index will be around \n105 or greater.\n\nAs the ridge is weakened slightly mid next week, isolated to \nscattered afternoon thunderstorms resume. Resultant cloud coverage \nmay help to moderate temperatures somewhat, though highs will remain \nin the upper 90s with heat index values reaching 103+ for a majority \nof north and central GA. These will slowly decline each day through \nthe end of next week. The prolonged heat will be a significant \nhazard. Overnight temps will stay in the mid to upper 70s.",
